====================================================================
DOMAIN: Engels and the Making of Orthodox Marxism (Second International)
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-11
PAPERS_FOUND: 13 total (High: 6, Medium: 5, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The literature on Engels's role in shaping orthodox Marxism centers on a
foundational question: to what degree did Engels's popularizing texts —
Anti-Dühring (1878), Socialism: Utopian and Scientific (1880), and Dialectics
of Nature (posthumous, 1925) — create "Marxism" as a systematic worldview
(Weltanschauung) independent of, and in some respects distorting, Marx's own
analytical method? Scholars broadly divide between those who treat Engels as
Marx's faithful expositor and those who see his work as inaugurating a
distinctive "Engelsian" paradigm that became the foundation of Second
International orthodoxy and eventually Soviet diamat.

The Second International period (1889–1914) saw Kautsky and Plekhanov develop
Engels's ideas into a scientific-determinist Marxism. Kautsky's "orthodox
Marxism" interpreted historical development as a natural-scientific law,
emphasizing the inevitable collapse of capitalism and the passive waiting
strategy of German Social Democracy. Plekhanov, the "father of Russian
Marxism," drew on Engels's Dialectics of Nature to construct dialectical
materialism as a complete philosophical system, providing the template that
Lenin and later Soviet Marxism would codify into the canonical "diamat." The
literature traces this genealogy from Engels through Plekhanov to Stalin's
codification, with particular attention to the role of Anti-Dühring and the
concept of laws of dialectics.

Recent scholarship has revisited these questions from multiple angles:
reassessments of Kautsky's historical thought (Gronow, Blackledge, Nygaard),
analysis of Plekhanov's philosophical contributions and their distortions
(Shaw, Oizerman, Sockol), critiques of the "Engels problem" framing itself
(Carver), and broader surveys of the Second International's theoretical
inheritance (Byron, Levine). There is growing attention to whether the
label "vulgar Marxism" applied to the Second International is itself a
retrospective construction that misrepresents genuine theoretical complexity.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is central to understanding how Engels's philosophy of history was
received, canonized, and transmitted — and thus to evaluating claims about
Engels's own philosophical positions versus what his successors made of them.
The question of Engels's "responsibility" for determinism and fatalism in
Second International Marxism directly bears on assessments of his departures
from and developments of Marx.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Specialized monograph-length studies of the line from Engels specifically
through Plekhanov to Soviet diamat remain rare in English-language scholarship;
most treatments are either brief articles or embedded in broader histories of
Marxism. The Tudor and Tudor edited volume on the revisionism debate (1988)
could not be verified through search scripts and is omitted.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
This domain should be synthesized alongside Domains 3 (Engels's dialectics)
and 6 (Western Marxist critiques of Engels), since the Second International
reception forms the immediate context for Western Marxism's "break" with
Engelsian orthodoxy.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Engels as originator of "Marxism as system": [4 papers] — Byron, Levine,
  Carver (Making Marx Marx), Smith — argue Engels created the philosophical
  and ideological framework that his successors used.
- Rehabilitation/reassessment of Kautsky: [4 papers] — Gronow, Blackledge
  (2006), Nygaard, Lih — challenge the standard caricature of Kautsky as
  mechanical determinist.
- Plekhanov and the diamat genealogy: [3 papers] — Shaw, Oizerman, Sockol —
  examine Plekhanov's mediation between Engels and Soviet philosophy.
====================================================================
}

@article{byron2022engelsianism,
  author = {Byron, Chris},
  title = {Engelsianism, Second Internationalism, and the Loss of {Marx}'s Critical Method},
  journal = {Critique},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {50},
  number = {4},
  pages = {535--556},
  doi = {10.1080/03017605.2023.2199584},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that the dominant understanding of "Marxism" as a system comprising Dialectical Materialism and Historical Materialism was produced by Engels and consolidated by Second International thinkers including Lenin and Trotsky. Marx himself never used either phrase and, Byron argues, explicitly rejected the content of these theories. Engels's writings on Capital provide the origin-point for both doctrines, and the Second International's limited access to Marx's own texts meant they depended on Engels's proclamations as their guide to "Marxism."

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the domain's central question — Engels as the creator of "Marxism" as a worldview — and provides a textual case for the divergence between Marx's critical method and the systematic philosophy Engels constructed. Important for assessing how Engels's philosophy of history was transmitted and transformed.

  POSITION: Represents the strong "Engels problem" thesis: Engels distorted Marx and the Second International perpetuated the distortion.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-Marx-divergence, Second-International, dialectical-materialism, orthodoxy, High},
  abstract = {This essay argues that the predominant view of ‘Marxism’ was largely shaped by Engels and the Second International. The predominant view of Marxism includes a metaphysical theory (Dialectical Materialism) and a Scientific Theory (Historical Materialism). Marx never used either phrase. Moreover, a close reading of his mature works and letters reveals that he explicitly rejected the content of those theories. Hence, in the shaping of ‘Marxism’, a series of confusions and inaccuracies about history and economic development were attributed to Marx. One source of miscommunication was Engels, who seems to have misunderstood Marx’s theories in Capital. Moreover, Engels’ writings provide the origin point for ‘Dialectical’ and ‘Historical Materialism’. These attributions to Marx were taken up by Lenin and Trotsky in the Second International. Unlike Engels, Lenin and the Second International had access to few of Marx’s writings, so they had to rely on Engels’ proclamations on Marx’s work for their understanding of ‘Marxism’. Subsequently, ‘Marxism’ has been perverted, as it relates to Marx’s actual method of analysis and presentation, in his critical engagement with capitalism. In this essay the author charts the miscommunication of Marx’s theories, and then provides the reader with Marx’s actual critical method for analyzing Capitalism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{gronow2016formation,
  author = {Gronow, Jukka},
  title = {On the Formation of {Marxism}: {Karl Kautsky}'s Theory of Capitalism, the {Marxism} of the {Second International} and {Karl Marx}'s Critique of Political Economy},
  year = {2016},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004306653},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Provides a systematic reconstruction of Kautsky's economic and political theory in relation to Marx's Critique of Political Economy, arguing that while Kautsky's political practice suffered from persistent weaknesses, his theory of capitalism — especially before 1910 — was more sophisticated than the standard caricature of mechanical determinism allows. Distinguishes between "Kautsky's Marxism" and "Marx's Marxism" as two different theoretical projects.

  RELEVANCE: The most detailed recent scholarly treatment of Kautsky's theoretical formation; establishes the intellectual content of the "orthodox Marxism" that Engels's texts helped constitute, and provides the detailed basis for evaluating Engels's influence on Second International theory.

  POSITION: Partial rehabilitation of Kautsky's theoretical sophistication while acknowledging the political limitations of his determinism.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Second-International, formation-of-Marxism, capitalism-theory, High},
  abstract = {Preface and Acknowledgements Introduction PART I: KAUTSKY'S MARXISM 1. Organised Capitalism, the General Cartel and the Proletariat 2. The Dispute over Revisionism 3. The Theory of Immiseration, Socialist Consciousness and the Intellectuals 4. Socialism as Science 5. The Capitalist Law of Appropriation: Kautsky's Interpretation of Karl Marx's Economic Thought 6. The Centralisation of Capital and Monopoly Formation 7. Imperialism and the Relation between Industrial and Agrarian Countries 8. Imperialism and Its Alternatives 9. Imperialism as the Last Stage of Capitalism 10. Theoretical Sources of Kautsky's and Lenin's Studies on Imperialism 11. Imperialism as the Truth about Capitalism 12. Parliamentary Democracy and Revolutionary Tactics 13. The Question of Democracy and Dictatorship: Lenin's Critique of Kautsky the Renegade PART II: MARX'S MARXISM 14. The Immanent Critique and the Natural Rights Theory 15. John Locke, Adam Smith and Karl Marx's Critique of Private Property 16. The Principle of Labour 17. The Theory of Increasing Misery and the Critique of Capitalism Conclusion Bibliography Index},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{blackledge2006kautsky,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Karl {Kautsky} and {Marxist} Historiography},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {70},
  number = {3},
  pages = {337--359},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.70.3.337},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Kautsky's historical writings — on the early Church, the Reformation, and contemporary social formations — to argue that his theory of history, especially before 1910, was sophisticated and powerful, not the mechanical economic determinism critics have assumed. The political weaknesses in Kautsky's Marxism are separated from its theoretical merits.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the evidential basis for distinguishing between Kautsky's actual theoretical positions and the "orthodox Marxist" label applied retrospectively; relevant to assessing how much of the Second International's "fatalism" can be traced to Engels's texts versus subsequent political pressures.

  POSITION: Kautsky rehabilitation camp; treats Second International Marxism as more theoretically nuanced than commonly supposed.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Marxist-historiography, Second-International, rehabilitation, High},
  abstract = {The traditional dismissive characterization of Karl Kautsky's Marxism as a form of mechanical or even Darwinian evolutionism is a caricature that obscures the very real and important contribution he made to historical materialism in the early years of the last century. Kautsky's historical studies of both the early Church and Reformation “communism,” as well as his attempts to analyze the contemporary Russian and American social formations, show that, while Kautsky's Marxism suffered from consistent political weaknesses, his theory of history, especially as it was articulated in the years before 1910, was sophisticated and powerful. While the power of Kautsky's Marxism became muted after this period, the weaknesses of his later works should not be allowed to obscure the insights contained within his earlier work; insights that informed some of the best work of the next generation of Marxists.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{nygaard2009constructing,
  author = {Nygaard, Bertel},
  title = {Constructing {Marxism}: {Karl Kautsky} and the {French Revolution}},
  journal = {History of European Ideas},
  year = {2009},
  volume = {35},
  number = {4},
  pages = {450--464},
  doi = {10.1016/j.histeuroideas.2009.04.002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Shows that Kautsky's writings on the French Revolution were crucial not only to constructing a Marxist interpretation of that event (which dominated 20th-century historiography) but to constructing "Marxism" itself as a comprehensive systematic theory with historical studies as its evidential base. Kautsky is seen as actively building Marxism as a Weltanschauung through historical work.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the process by which "Marxism" was constituted as a worldview through the theoretical labor of Second International figures — the same process in which Engels's texts (especially Anti-Dühring) were used as the methodological foundation. Directly connects to the question of how Engelsian categories shaped the practice of Marxist historiography.

  POSITION: Treats Kautsky as a constructive (rather than merely derivative) theorist of orthodox Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Marxism-construction, French-Revolution, Weltanschauung, High},
  abstract = {Karl Kautsky's writings on the French Revolution were crucial to the construction not only of the Marxist interpretation of the Revolution, which was perhaps the most important reference point for the historiography of that event during the 20th century, but even of Marxism itself as a comprehensive, systematic theory partly based on historical studies. However, these writings have been neglected and practically forgotten for decades, mainly because of the general rejection of Kautsky's theories after the October Revolution of 1917, in Marxist as well as non-Marxist circles. Studying these writings, spanning roughly four decades from 1889 till 1930, we may see dynamic interrelations between historical study, theory construction and contemporary political intervention. Kautsky's approach to such key Marxist concepts as class and state prove to be much more subtle and nuanced than what has commonly been assumed, incorporating the results of historical study rather than pure social theory. Yet, his account does contain important internal tensions and contradictions between agency and objective conditions and between the historical material and the normative and political perspective of the historiographer. Several of these internal tensions were carried on into mainstream Marxist accounts of the Revolution, with important historiographical as well as political consequences.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{lih2018kautsky,
  author = {Lih, Lars T.},
  title = {The tasks of our times: {Kautsky}'s {Road to Power} in {Germany} and {Russia}},
  journal = {Studies in East European Thought},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {70},
  number = {2-3},
  pages = {121--140},
  doi = {10.1007/s11212-018-9308-2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Analyzes the reception of Kautsky's Road to Power (1909) in Germany and Russia, arguing that the text was received in fundamentally different ways: as an attack on reformism in Germany, and as a template for revolutionary strategy in Russia. Shows how the same text functioned differently in distinct political contexts, complicating simple accounts of "orthodox Kautskyism."

  RELEVANCE: The differential reception of a canonical Second International text shows how Engelsian and Kautskyan orthodoxy was appropriated and transformed in different national contexts, directly relevant to tracing the line from Engels through Kautsky to Bolshevism.

  POSITION: Revisionist account that complicates the standard narrative of Kautsky as a passive determinist.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Road-to-Power, Second-International, Russia, reception, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{levine2007corruption,
  author = {Levine, Norman},
  title = {Corruption and Fate of Left-Wing {Hegelianism}},
  journal = {Critique},
  year = {2007},
  volume = {35},
  number = {1},
  pages = {79--102},
  doi = {10.1080/03017600701238489},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Traces the "corruption" of the Left-Hegelian philosophical tradition through Engels's systematization of Marx's thought into a quasi-naturalistic dialectics, arguing that Engels imposed a Feuerbachian materialism onto what had been a more properly Hegelian method in Marx's mature work. This corruption passed through Plekhanov and Kautsky into Soviet diamat.

  RELEVANCE: One of the clearest formulations of the philosophical genealogy from Engels through the Second International to Soviet Marxism, with attention to the Left-Hegelian context. Directly relevant to assessing Engels's philosophical departures from Marx and their long-term consequences.

  POSITION: Strong "Engels problem" position: Engels's systematization corrupted the original Hegelian-critical impulse in Marx.
  },
  keywords = {Hegelianism, Engels-distortion, Plekhanov, diamat-genealogy, High},
  abstract = {Friedrich Engels introduced two fundamental distortions to Karl Marx's original theory. Engels was the inventor of dialectical materialism. Influenced by the development of 19th-century natural science, his close friend Karl Schorlemmer was a chemist, handicapped by a sophomoric understanding of Hegelian philosophy, Engels fused dialectic and the physical sciences. Dialectical materialism, an Engelsian invention, maintained that nature, and history, functioned in terms of dialectical principles. Second, under the Stalinist system this Engelsian distortion became the philosophic foundation, Diamat, of Soviet Marxism. The dialectical materialism of Engels was turned into a metaphysics and was used as an instrument to validate the dictatorship of Stalin. The original message of Marx was maimed by this Engelsian intervention and only the collapse of the Soviet Union opened the opportunity to rethink Marx's original position and to cleanse Marxism from this Engelsian debasement.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{shaw1988plekhanov,
  author = {Shaw, William H.},
  title = {Plekhanov on the role of the individual in history},
  journal = {Studies in Soviet Thought},
  year = {1988},
  volume = {35},
  number = {3},
  pages = {247--265},
  doi = {10.1007/bf01044986},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Plekhanov's account of the individual in history — particularly his claim in The Role of the Individual in History (1898) that great individuals are the product of their times rather than its cause — and analyzes the tension between this determinism and Plekhanov's own active political role. Discusses how Plekhanov attempted to reconcile socialist determinism with political agency.

  RELEVANCE: Plekhanov's treatment of history is the direct link between Engels's philosophy of history (especially its naturalistic-determinist strain) and Russian/Soviet Marxism. Shaw's analysis clarifies the specific doctrinal content of that link and its tensions with political practice.

  POSITION: Analytical reconstruction; identifies both the strengths and internal tensions of Plekhanov's determinist philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, role-of-individual, determinism, diamat-genealogy, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{oizerman2017dialectical,
  author = {Oizerman, Teodor I.},
  title = {On a Critical Reflection on Dialectical Materialism},
  journal = {Russian Studies in Philosophy},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {55},
  number = {2},
  pages = {98--121},
  doi = {10.1080/10611967.2017.1313653},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A critical internal assessment of dialectical materialism by a leading Russian philosopher, arguing that while Engels and Plekhanov provided the foundation for the doctrine, its subsequent Soviet codification under Stalin involved significant philosophical impoverishment. Oizerman distinguishes the genuine philosophical content in Engels and Plekhanov from the dogmatic schema that became "diamat" in the Stalin period.

  RELEVANCE: Provides an authoritative internal assessment of the Engels-Plekhanov-Lenin-Stalin line of philosophical development, identifying where genuine philosophical work ends and doctrinal codification begins. Important for understanding what was at stake in the creation of Soviet diamat.

  POSITION: Nuanced internal critique: distinguishes genuine philosophical contributions in the Engels-Plekhanov tradition from later dogmatic codification.
  },
  keywords = {dialectical-materialism, Engels, Plekhanov, Soviet-philosophy, Stalin, Medium},
  abstract = {This article offers an analysis of dialectical materialism. The author, being a supporter of this theory, offers a self-critical assessment of its foundations. He argues that the predecessors of Marxism constructed their systems with the confidence that they were building the true and only true philosophy. This utopian idea shared by Marx, Engels, and their successors was refuted by subsequent developments in philosophy. Indeed, philosophy by its very nature is pluralistic and interminable. Self-critical Marxism must recognize the legitimacy not only of dialectical materialism, but also of other philosophies no matter how much they may differ in their content. This is supported by analysis of the main categories and problems of dialectical materialism—the laws of dialectics, matter, evolution, consciousness, and truth.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{sockol2023plekhanov,
  author = {Sockol, David},
  title = {Verso of the {``Vulgar''} Portrait: {Georgi Plekhanov}'s Anti-{Kantianism} and Activist Aesthetic},
  journal = {Rethinking Marxism},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {35},
  number = {4},
  pages = {484--501},
  doi = {10.1080/08935696.2023.2251333},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Challenges the standard portrayal of Plekhanov as a crude "vulgar Marxist" by examining his anti-Kantian philosophical polemics and his aesthetics. Argues that Plekhanov's work, while indebted to Engels's naturalistic materialism, contains a more nuanced aesthetic theory and philosophical program than the "vulgar Marxism" label suggests.

  RELEVANCE: Relevant to understanding the complexity of the Engelsian legacy in Plekhanov: the same move that labels Plekhanov "vulgar" often functions to contain criticism of Engels. Sockol's rehabilitation parallels the Kautsky-rehabilitation literature and raises similar questions about retrospective distortion.

  POSITION: Revisionist; challenges the "vulgar Marxism" framework as applied to Plekhanov.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, vulgar-Marxism, aesthetics, anti-Kantianism, rehabilitation, Medium},
  abstract = {This essay examines Georgi Plekhanov, founder of the Russian Marxist movement and a supposed exemplar of “vulgar Marxism.” It analyses Plekhanov's formulation of a Marxist aesthetic, interrogating the widespread image of Plekhanov's “vulgar” focus on economic determinism and his neglect of cultural forces' influence on revolutionary praxis, locating him instead within a fuller history of Marxist thought. A contextualization of Plekhanov's fin-de-siecle writings on art demonstrates that these texts were effectively a component of his contemporary polemics against critics of Marxist theory, and that within them he was pursuing his polemical agenda of criticizing Kantian philosophy and defending Marxist materialism. These dual aims manifested in Plekhanov's rejection of the Kantian notion of aesthetic disinterestedness and a stress on the formative role class conflict exerted on artistic creation, ultimately resulting in his formulation of an original aesthetic theory prescribing political commitment and thus mobilizing artworks towards revolutionary ends.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{carver2017making,
  author = {Carver, Terrell},
  title = {Making {Marx} {Marx}},
  journal = {Journal of Classical Sociology},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {17},
  number = {1},
  pages = {10--27},
  doi = {10.1177/1468795x17691388},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that "Marx" as a canonical figure — the author of a unified body of doctrine — was substantially constructed by Engels through his editorial and popularizing work after Marx's death. The "Marx" that readers and political movements encountered was largely Engels's creation: his selection, framing, and systematization of texts determined what counted as "Marxist."

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most direct formulation of Engels's role in creating the "Marxism" that the Second International received and developed. Directly relevant to the question of Engels's responsibility for orthodoxy, since it locates that responsibility in the textual politics of canon-formation.

  POSITION: Strong constructivist thesis: "Marxism" as doctrine is primarily Engels's construction rather than a reflection of Marx's actual positions.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-Marx-construction, canon-formation, Marxism-origins, High},
  abstract = {The context through which readers have come to know Marx as ‘Marx’ was not discovered. It was constructed by Marx in the first instance, and by others during his lifetime and afterwards. What he told us about himself at different stages of his life has been assimilated in various ways to reinforce what more-or-less authorised biographers think we should know about his ‘life and thought’. These accounts are based on what bibliographers present as his ‘collected works’, graded by significance. Intellectual biographies are couched in a genre that is neither the contingent everyday (since readers are presented with a life story that has ended) nor the publicity-minded autobiographical (as Marx’s self-characterisations certainly were). Marx himself posted a public notice in 1847, putting a project in self-publicity underway. His own autobiographical and auto-bibliographical Preface of 1859, and Engels’ summarising review of Marx’s book of the same year, both had somewhat wider contemporary audiences. In recounting his activities and works, Marx’s inclusions and emphases are quite different from twentieth- and twenty-first-century canons. A Collected Essays project for Marx was mooted by himself in the early 1850s, but the plan was to recirculate only those items which would raise issues that were still politically current. In 1872, a number of German socialists consciously embarked on a political process of constructing Marx and Engels as iconic founding fathers. Their mass recirculation of The Communist Manifesto sparked an enormous number of reprints and translations. This highly readable text made Marx ‘great’. After his death in 1883, the situation changed dramatically. Engels’ republication of Marx’s works with new introductions and prefaces, along with Engels’ own works, promoted Engels’ projects and ideas as following directly from, and intentionally supplementary to, Marx’s ‘thought’. Franz Mehring’s 1902 catalogue listing of the Marx–Engels archival legacy laid the basis for the first biography of Marx, making him a man of ‘great works’. After a false start in 1911–1913, a collected works project was undertaken in the early 1920s by D.B. Riazanov, resulting in 11 volumes of the Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe, discontinued at the outset of World War II. The revivified Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe project of the 1970s to date differs somewhat from Riazanov’s plan and methodology, but the overall outlines are self-consciously similar: works (including some manuscript and discarded ‘works’), ‘economic’ works, correspondence, notebooks and marginalia. These editorial presumptions make Marx and Engels ‘major’ and their interlocutors ‘minor’ and create hierarchies of genre and content. Uniform typographical presentation makes all writings look the same on the page or screen, and with indexing it becomes easy to read Marx as a ‘thinker’ delivering items of ‘thought’. Thus, editorial practices have worked to mystify the ‘everyday Marx’ and to erase vital and productive ways to think about politics, activism and struggle.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{carver2020engelsttoday,
  author = {Carver, Terrell},
  title = {Engels Today: An Introduction},
  journal = {International Critical Thought},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {10},
  number = {3},
  pages = {323--330},
  doi = {10.1080/21598282.2020.1834685},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Surveys the state of Engels scholarship on the bicentenary of his birth, reviewing debates about his relationship to Marx, his role in forming the Marxist tradition, and his continuing significance. Carver identifies the key interpretive fault lines — the "Engels problem," the question of his originality, his political legacy — as unresolved but clarified by recent scholarship.

  RELEVANCE: Useful orientation to the state of the debate on Engels's role in constructing Marxism; situates the orthodox-Marxism question within the broader landscape of Engels studies and identifies which controversies remain open.

  POSITION: Carver's own constructivist position (Engels made "Marxism") contextualizes his overview, but the article is primarily a state-of-the-field survey.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-reception, Engels-problem, state-of-field, Medium},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT Engels today is a very large archive of texts, which generate many significant puzzles. However, most factual accounts of his life, his partnership with Marx, and his political activities are actually answers to questions that are very seldom posed directly. This article poses searching questions about the Marx-centric formation of the Engels archive, the meaning of senior/junior roles in the partnership, the nature of Marxism in relation to Marx and Engels as independent thinkers, and the commonplace presumptions of consistency and certainty characteristic of historical commentary. It reviews the five articles in this collection in order to suggest a novel “Engels-centric” approach to intellectual biography and scholarly evaluation. An “Engels-centric” approach requires a fresh look at the materials we have, as well as a search for further contextual documentation, in particular from “third party” connections. The result would be a different view of Engels’s life and activities prior to his collaboration with Marx, of the wider political context in which he collaborated with Marx, and of his independent intellectual achievements understood in a less Marx-centric way. An “Engels-centric” approach would give this important figure a new lease of after-life.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{blackledge2019politics,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {{Engels}'s Politics: Strategy and Tactics after 1848},
  journal = {Socialism and Democracy},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {33},
  number = {2},
  pages = {23--45},
  doi = {10.1080/08854300.2019.1644579},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Engels's political strategy and tactics after 1848, arguing against the reduction of his thought to mechanical determinism or fatalism. Blackledge shows that Engels combined a long-term historical perspective on capitalism's development with concrete attention to political opportunity and socialist organization, and that his relationship to the Second International was more critical and nuanced than often assumed.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses whether Engels was responsible for the fatalism and passivity associated with Second International orthodox Marxism, by examining his actual political writings and strategic arguments. Important for distinguishing Engels's own positions from the uses to which his successors put them.

  POSITION: Defends Engels against the charge of mechanical determinism while acknowledging tensions in his political thought.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-politics, strategy, fatalism, Second-International, Medium},
  abstract = {While Friedrich Engels’s The Condition of the English Working Class is almost universally praised as an honest, insightful and indeed profound picture of working-class life in Manchester in the 1840s, much of the rest of his contribution to Marxism has typically been dismissed as popularization that reduced Marx’s sublation of materialism and idealism to a warmed-over version of eighteenth-century materialism. What is more, it is this “Engelsian”Marxism that is said to have informed the mechanical and authoritarian interpretations of Marxist politics that became hegemonic on the twentieth-century left. Criticisms of Engels’s politics have a long pedigree. At the end of the nineteenth century, Eduard Bernstein famously argued that though Engels had in practice embraced modern, constructive, evolutionary and electoral politics he failed to theorize this break with the older destructive and insurrectionary form he and Marx had embraced in the 1840s. Bernstein imagined his own “revisionism” as an attempt to draw the logical theoretical conclusions from Engels’s mature political practice to update socialist theory to modern conditions. Though Bernstein’s critique of Marxism cannot bear critical scrutiny, something like his crude contrast between constructive and destructive or evolutionary and insurrectionary politics has tended to frame even some of the best twentieth-century academic interpretations},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{smith2009antiutopianism,
  author = {Smith, Paul B.},
  title = {Reflections on Aspects of {Marxist} Anti-Utopianism},
  journal = {Critique},
  year = {2009},
  volume = {37},
  number = {1},
  pages = {99--120},
  doi = {10.1080/03017600802598260},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the anti-utopian strand in Marxist thought — the insistence that socialism is "scientific" rather than utopian — tracing it from Marx and Engels through Kautsky and Bernstein to later Marxism. Argues that the Marxist critique of utopianism, while philosophically motivated, carried costs in terms of political imagination and created a fatalism about history that undermined activist politics.

  RELEVANCE: Connects Engels's Socialism: Utopian and Scientific (his most widely-read popularizing text) to the broader pattern of Second International determinism, showing how the "scientific socialism" framing contributed to the fatalist tendency that critics associate with orthodox Marxism.

  POSITION: Critical of the anti-utopian strand; argues that the scientific/utopian dichotomy in Engels and Kautsky had damaging political consequences.
  },
  keywords = {scientific-socialism, anti-utopianism, Kautsky, Bernstein, Engels-popularization, Low},
  abstract = {This article argues that Marx and Engels’ anti-utopianism was consistent and based on objective political and economic criteria. Marx and Engels were critical of the ideas and practices of followers of the early founders of socialist theory. Politically, they argued these followers were sectarian. Theoretically, a utopian understanding of political economy was partial and pseudo-scientific. This explained how the means sectarians promoted for achieving the socialist goal were ineffective. Utopianism consisted of a mismatch between ends and means. It was a form of unrealisable socialism. Marx and Engels did not try to prevent people thinking imaginatively about the classless society of the future. This is a distortion. It originated with Bernstein's revisionism. Stalinism and bourgeois sociology consolidated this misunderstanding. As a result anti-utopian Marxism has appeared to be an inconsistent form of conservative subjectivity.},
